00:03:47.098 --> 00:03:48.640
How many of you are
actually planning

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.640
to migrate to the cloud?

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.380
I don't know if I
can see everyone,

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:58.670
but I could see some hands
are rising, so it's good.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.230
So first, I'll show you
the platform that we

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.530
have before the migration.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:08.170
And at a high level, this is
our infrastructure footprint.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:12.460
And if you look at it, we
have 27-node Teradata cluster

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:18.459
with 120-terabyte disk
space and 22 Hadoop

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.100
nodes of 1-petabyte disk space.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.330
And also, we have a
[? 60P ?] Informatica server,

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.850
we have automation
engine, Tableau,

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.750
which is our BI reporting
and visualization tool.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.740
And we have data products
wherein we create custom

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.890
dashboards which cannot
be done through Tableau.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.420
So this is our footprint
at a high level.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.560
And if you look at the
data flow, the data

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.400
we acquire through internal
and external APIs, and also

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:54.580
internal databases, we acquire
into HDR files and also NFS

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.880
and process through
Hive and Teradata.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.800
And we also copy most recent
data from Hive to Teradata

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.330
almost daily basis.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:08.500
This is mainly because of the
database constraints we have--

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.040
size constraints on Teradata.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:16.750
And also, if you look at
Tableau and other data product

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.660
applications, we have the
choice of querying either Hive

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.690
or Teradata, so there is a
flexibility our analysts have.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:32.350
And if you look at some of the
challenges that we are having,

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.280
scale.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:37.550
If you are familiar with
database appliances,

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.640
it is not easy to increase
the size without going

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.120
through a lot of effort.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.770
It requires a lot of money,
resources, and it's not easy.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.240
And same thing with Hadoop.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.370
We have to plan a lot.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:54.650
And if you look at
SLAs, in the past,

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:57.920
we had frequent delays
in SLAs and sometimes, we

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:01.210
were delayed by
four to six hours.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.000
And for us, data is
critical because ours

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.570
is a media company, and we
are not short of major events.

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:13.400
We have major events throughout
the year starting in January

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.110
all the way to December.

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:18.890
And we start with the CES, and
then March Madness, Super Bowl,

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:22.850
a lot of news events and
a lot of other all access

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:26.030
original launches, and
a lot of other events.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.760
So there are a lot of
problems with the SLAs,

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.600
so that is one of the challenge.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:38.040
And SQL, I'm sure
some of you also

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.180
might have in your companies.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.300
We have a few users
who could bring down

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.110
the database to its knees.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:49.020
And of course, it
is not by intention.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.270
Everybody writes inefficient
sequence at times,

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:53.880
even I do too.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.590
So whenever those
inefficient sequences

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:59.950
are going into Teradata, the
performance is going down.

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:02.830
And then we do the
backups almost every week.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:05.100
And whenever the
backups are happening,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.230
the performance is degraded.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:10.590
And every Monday, we
always constantly have

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:14.010
to monitor how the
reports are coming along

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:17.050
because we have seen most
of the delays on Mondays.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.540
And also the concurrent users.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:23.290
If the number of users are
increased on the system,

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.320
it is typical with some of
these [INAUDIBLE] databases

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.610
that the database
performance will go down.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:31.370
So these are some of the
challenges that we are having.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.790
And right around
that time, there

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:37.360
was a company-wide
cloud-first initiative

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.890
which helped us
to actually start

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:41.500
thinking about cloud migration.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:45.640
And also, CBSI and
Google are good partners.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:48.590
We have been using Google's
products-- for example,

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:51.610
we have been using DFP,
which is DART For Publishers,

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.260
and also G Suite.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.860
So it was a natural
evolution for us

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:59.750
and it was the right fit to
start looking at Google Cloud,

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:02.680
and that is how we
started our migration.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:06.620
So let us see how we
actually did the migration.

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:10.720
We considered a few options
before going into BigQuery.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.820
So option number one is
migrate only data to BigQuery.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:19.420
And option number two,
migrate entire platform,

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:23.110
meaning infrastructure,
processes, data--

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:24.500
everything to cloud.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:28.030
However, this option
number two sounds

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:31.210
good, but with the given
time and resources,

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.429
we thought that it is
not possible to do it.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:38.120
So we looked at a middle ground,
which is option number three.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.780
So now let us look at
what that option three is

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.600
and what are some of
details of option three.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:46.360
So migrate data
to cloud, meaning

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:49.300
wherever the data
is processed, we

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:51.460
have to have the
data in the cloud.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.770
That way, all the
users will have access

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:57.880
to one single database
for their analysis.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:00.530
And second, Big Data.

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:06.062
So we thought that processing
the Big Data in the cloud

00:09:06.062 --> 00:09:07.520
would help us a
lot because that is

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.770
where we have most
of the challenges,

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:12.050
and we tackled that first.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:14.750
And when I say Big Data,
mainly around click

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:20.720
stream data, which is the web
traffic and ad impression data.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.110
And also, there were no
technical constraints for us

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:27.230
not to migrate the
processes into the cloud,

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:31.430
so it made it easy for
making the decision.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:34.730
While we did that, we also
changed the data structures

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.850
for these big data
sets, and that helped

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:40.550
us a lot with performance.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:43.340
And of course, we did
this in consultation

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:46.610
with a task force
which was formed

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:49.250
by inviting some of
the key stakeholders

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:52.780
from our businesses, and
that worked out pretty good.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.670
And I'll give you some
more details later on.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:00.960
And also, if you look at
the dimension processing,

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:01.740
it is not--

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:05.580
while BigQuery is very good
and high-performance database,

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.560
we cannot do traditional--

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:12.990
the DMLs like we do with
traditional databases.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.730
Whereas Informatica
process that we

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.600
had had a lot of
in-service updates,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:21.210
deletes on a daily basis.

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.850
We couldn't do that, so
we looked at Informatica

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.440
replacement at that time.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.830
However, we couldn't
find a good replacement.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:31.470
And also, with the given
time and resources,

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.590
we decided, OK, we are going
to keep Informatica for now

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:35.520
on-premise.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:40.045
However, copy all the
process data to the cloud

00:10:40.045 --> 00:10:41.670
because ultimately,
the goal is to have

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.390
all the data in the cloud.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:48.350
We also decided to keep all
the Hadoop jobs on-premise.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:53.030
However, we made a simple change
by writing the output files

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:56.040
not only to Hadoop,
but also to GCS.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:01.850
And this gave us a leverage to
reduce the time of migration.

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:06.040
Also, at the time of
decommissioning Hadoop,

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.300
it made it easy for us.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.680
Data product applications.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.500
We decided to migrate the
application code first,

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.980
while keeping the
Teradata on-premise.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:21.580
Because while the
code is in the cloud,

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.290
the applications were talking
to the database on the premise.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.420
So we did that because
we wanted to make

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:30.810
simultaneous progress
with multiple,

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:33.130
I would say, lanes, projects.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.870
So those are the
[INAUDIBLE] we took.

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:38.370
And likewise, the BI
reporting servers,

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.170
we kept them on-premise.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.163
This is also purely
because of time.

00:11:42.163 --> 00:11:43.830
Otherwise, there is
no technical reason.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:47.680
So that also we kept on-premise.

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:54.250
And the next one, I'll show
you the timeline and how

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:56.140
all this migration took place.

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:58.990
So we start in March 2017.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:01.660
That's when we started planning
and started looking at all

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:05.530
the new tools and technologies
because we are going into a new

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:07.690
platform-- brand new platform--

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:10.180
and we need to evaluate
what is out there

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:13.130
and what we need to do
with all the new tools.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:16.680
So it took us about six
months, and by August 2017,

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.260
we are actually ready to
start migrating or start

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:22.670
working on the actual code.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:26.290
So from August
through January, we

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:29.720
have migrated the code that is
required for the first business

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:30.220
unit.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:35.320
So by January 2018, our
first BU is ready to go live.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:37.450
And as you can
see at the bottom,

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:41.020
it took us about 11 months
for the first site to go live,

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:43.190
which is amazing.

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:47.960
And then, by May, we are
live with all the business

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:50.210
units in BigQuery.

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:53.950
All of this took 15 months.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.370
And then, if you
look at July 2018,

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.670
that is when we shut
down the Teradata.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:03.720
So we kept running the
Teradata between May and July

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.210
for about two months
just as a fallback.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:09.030
In case, if anybody has
any doubt on anything,

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:10.210
they can always go back.

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:14.820
But in July, we started
shutting down the Teradata.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:20.400
And between July and December,
we converted Hadoop jobs.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:27.150
And then, by December, we
were able to shut down Hadoop.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:32.070
So all this has
happened over 22 months.

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:37.350
And as you can see, we have
supported three different data

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:39.960
platforms over an
extended period

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.550
of time, which is not easy.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:47.490
And also, we had to migrate
this data over many, many brands

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:50.130
where the priorities are
different for each business

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.550
unit.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:54.010
And also, we did
all this with a team

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:57.430
of eight dedicated
engineers with some help

00:13:57.430 --> 00:13:59.920
from other engineers
across the company.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.200
And it was a great
team effort and we

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.990
have excellent stakeholders
who have helped us

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.930
with a lot of testing
early on, and overall it

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:09.470
was a great team effort.

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:12.580
So now, I'm going
to hand off to Dan,

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:16.240
who will talk to you about
some of the technical details

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:17.830
of the migration.

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:19.320
Dan?

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:23.650
DANIEL LESCOHIER: OK, so
I'll cover the Google Cloud

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:27.460
architecture that replaced
the on-prem systems that we're

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:32.320
retiring, the migration
strategies for our big data

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:38.230
acquisition jobs, our ETL
jobs, and our historical data,

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:40.690
and the technical
challenges that we

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:43.580
encountered during the project.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:48.000
So the scope of the project was
to retire Hadoop and Teradata

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:52.370
and move our data
products to the cloud.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:55.730
So Hadoop and Teradata
are going to be gone, so

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:57.130
what do we replace them with?

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:03.910
So for the big data
acquisition scripts,

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:06.310
they used to write to the
Hadoop Distributed File System

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:09.010
because we needed a
large distributed file

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:10.630
system to store it on.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:16.810
So we replaced that
on the GCP platform

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:20.980
with an Apache NiFi cluster that
we set up on the Google Compute

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:25.090
Engine, and it would
write its data to the GCS.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:28.060
I'll cover these systems
in more detail soon.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.230
For our big data
transformation jobs,

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:36.570
we replaced our
Hadoop map reduce jobs

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:38.955
with Apache Beam
on Google Dataflow.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.830
For Hive, Impala, and
Teradata, they all

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:47.120
got replaced with BigQuery.

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:54.770
Our Informatica jobs
were writing to Teradata,

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:59.030
so to replace Teradata,
we wrote the results

00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:01.700
to an on-prem Postgres database.

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:05.660
And then we used the
Apache NiFi cluster on GCE

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:09.180
to copy the data
daily to BigQuery.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.210
For our big data
acquisition jobs, as I said,

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:17.250
we replaced it with an
Apache NiFi cluster on GCE.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:21.210
And we installed those
as Docker containers

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:25.770
on Google's
container-optimized OS.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:31.560
So the way it works is that the
vendor places their data files

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:38.100
on GCS every hour, and we have
a workflow running continuously

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:43.420
in Apache NiFi listing new
files that appear in the bucket,

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:45.780
fetching them,
doing pre-processing

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:49.230
on them, which is mainly
decompressing the data,

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:54.870
and writing it onto our own
GCS buckets for later processes

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.540
to work on.

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:01.350
And the NiFi workflows
also subscribe

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:07.599
to a Pub/Sub topic that accepts
commands for re-acquiring data.

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:11.250
In case there are
operational issues,

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:15.099
our operators can issue commands
through the NiFi cluster

00:17:15.099 --> 00:17:17.829
to reacquire data.

00:17:17.829 --> 00:17:25.690
So for our ETL that was running
as Hadoop map reduce jobs

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:28.660
as Python programs.

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:31.960
We wanted to reuse a lot of
the transformation functions

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:35.170
that we had on the
on-prem systems,

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:42.550
so we made a decision to migrate
to the Apache Beam Python SDK

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:46.730
and run it on the
Google Dataflow service.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:52.180
So in order to do so, we
created agent machines

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:55.930
on Google Compute Engine that
has our automation engine

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:57.170
agent.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:01.180
It's also running on Google
Container-Optimized OS,

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:04.910
so the agent is running as a
service in a Docker container

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:08.770
and accepts patch job commands
from our on-prem automation

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.690
engine server.

00:18:10.690 --> 00:18:16.060
And those batch jobs launch a
container for each batch job

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:20.050
from application images
installed on the agent machine.

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:23.710
And each of those batch jobs
are data flow client jobs

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:27.430
that issue commands to the
Google Dataflow service

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:30.170
to run batch jobs
to process the data.

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:34.630
So it's taking the data
that NiFi had placed on GCS,

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:39.370
transforming it, and
putting the results in GCS.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.910
So part of our Google
DoubleClick ad data

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:48.490
transformations is denormalizing
the data, looking up 20 fields

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:50.470
from nine tables.

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:53.080
We found it was hard
to write and maintain

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:57.340
in Python and
Dataflow, but it was

00:18:57.340 --> 00:19:01.420
easy to write it as BigQuery
queries just having nine

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:04.020
left outer joins in the query.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:06.430
And in our testing, we
found that it ran really

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:09.820
efficiently in BigQuery,
so that's what we're

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:12.050
using in production today.

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:16.840
And in the future, we are
preferring ELT and BigQuery

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:22.480
in most cases since it's
easier to write and maintain.

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:24.010
So another part
of the project is

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:26.380
that we needed to
get years of data

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:28.840
on the order of
hundreds of terabytes

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:33.940
from on-prem to
GCS and BigQuery.

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:36.850
So for data that's
only in Teradata,

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:40.060
we first copied
the data to Hadoop.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:44.920
Once the data is on HDFS, we
can use Google's Cloud Storage

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:50.140
Connector, and we installed that
on our on-prem Hadoop cluster

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:54.660
to copy the data in
parallel up to GCS.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:58.150
So we installed our GCS--

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:02.650
our Google service account
files on our client machines,

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:04.690
and then our on-prem
Hadoop cluster

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:07.450
could write the data to the GCS.

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:10.030
And it was extremely fast.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:12.610
If we wanted to, we could
have copied all of our data

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:15.560
in just a few days.

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.080
Also, at that time,
BigQuery did not

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:23.960
support the parquet file format
for data loads to BigQuery.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:26.900
So we created a
system that we could

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:31.400
write configuration files of
Hive queries for each table

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.060
that we needed to convert.

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:35.780
And those Hive queries
would convert the data types

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:39.220
and write Avro files on HDFS.

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:41.300
And we wrote
automation workflows

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:46.910
to do daily jobs to write
these Avro files on HDFS,

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:51.800
use the Hadoop DistCp command
to copy and parallel the GCS,

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:55.510
making use of Google's
Cloud Storage Connector,

00:20:55.510 --> 00:21:00.070
and running the BQ load commands
to get it into BigQuery.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:05.200
So for Informatica, we needed
to replace all the workflows

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:10.030
that target Teradata since
Teradata will be retired.

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:13.630
So we couldn't target
BigQuery directly

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:15.730
because all of those
jobs are written

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:19.850
as transactional
inserts and updates.

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:23.380
So what we decided to do is
we duplicated the workflows

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:29.020
and targeted them to an
on-prem Postgresql database.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:32.590
It was on prem because of
limitations in the database

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:36.320
drivers we had that had
write latency issues.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:38.320
So since Informatica
was on-prem,

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:42.590
the Postgresql database
also had to be on-prem.

00:21:42.590 --> 00:21:47.170
So to get it into BigQuery,
we used our NiFi cluster

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:49.270
on Google Compute Engine.

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:52.480
We created workflows
that accepted commands

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:55.090
from Google Pub/Sub,
those commands

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:58.900
saying to copy tables from
the on-prem Postgresql

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:01.210
database to BigQuery.

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:03.400
And we added those
table copy jobs

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:08.120
to our daily schedules
and our automation engine.

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:12.430
We also created global flags
in our automation engine

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:16.330
for Teradata retirement,
and added preconditions

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:19.780
to our Teradata
workflows on those flags.

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:24.400
So just by changing one
flag, we can turn on and off

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:27.310
all of those Teradata workflows.

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:30.070
So now I'll cover some of
the technical challenges

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:34.800
we encountered during
the migration project.

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:37.980
So as I explained, we were
using this complicated system

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:39.810
of getting the
data into Postgres,

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:43.470
and then copying it once a
day using our NiFi cluster,

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:46.180
so we'd like to simplify
that in the future.

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:50.520
So we've written a
library of reusable code

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:54.660
for getting slowly changing
dimension data directly

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:56.990
into BigQuery.

00:22:56.990 --> 00:23:00.930
The way the system works is
that new and updated records

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:04.720
are appended to log
tables in BigQuery.

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:08.550
The sources could either
be daily batch jobs that

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:13.650
append records from recent days
or could be real-time streaming

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:17.130
jobs that append to log tables.

00:23:17.130 --> 00:23:21.030
And both sources are
subject to duplicates,

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:26.040
so we wrote this templatized
BigQuery SQL that

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:31.050
reads from the log tables and
creates new versions of slowly

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:33.090
changing dimension tables.

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:35.700
Two types of those
tables, Type 1

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:39.450
for writing the current
version of the record

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:43.710
to the destination table,
and Type 2, which is writing

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:46.950
deduplicated history
records for each key

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:49.440
to the destination table.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.470
So the main parameters
for this library

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:57.300
is what's the source log
table that we're reading from,

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:00.330
what's the key for
this table, and what

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.420
are the fields that identify
the ordering or record

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:05.850
versions for that key.

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:09.810
And from that, we create
an order_by clause

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:13.980
with the most
recent record first.

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:19.500
So this is the basic template
for a Type 1 dimension table,

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.650
writing the current latest
version of the record

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:24.410
to the destination table.

00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:29.580
You can see it's making use of
BigQuery's ARRAY_AGG functions,

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:32.640
ordering by that ORDER
BY clause we generated,

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:35.890
and picking the
most recent record.

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:41.270
And that replaces the
destination table.

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:46.880
For Type 2 dimension tables, we
write the deduplicated history,

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:52.430
and we add history fields
of effective start and end

00:24:52.430 --> 00:24:56.060
timestamp of the record
in a third field, which

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:59.330
is a flag field indicating
whether this record is

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:01.730
the current record for this key.

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:04.880
So this is making use of
the windowing functions

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:06.490
that BigQuery supports.

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:10.430
You can see we're generating the
three extra fields I mentioned

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:14.280
and deduplicating the records.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:19.980
Another problem we had to solve
is having atomic isolated data

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:24.420
sets for our Dataflow jobs.

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:28.820
So we needed the data
processed by Dataflow

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:32.870
to be atomic so that
downstream jobs see

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:36.130
just the complete
output or no output.

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:39.560
If a data flow job fails, we
don't want downstream jobs

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:41.540
to see partial data.

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:43.520
And it needs to be isolated.

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:46.650
If two concurrent jobs are
running at the same time,

00:25:46.650 --> 00:25:49.360
we don't want them to be
using the same file names

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:51.770
and stepping on each other.

00:25:51.770 --> 00:25:57.410
So GCS has ACID guarantees
for individual file objects,

00:25:57.410 --> 00:26:00.850
but Dataflow runs in parallel
and produces a whole data

00:26:00.850 --> 00:26:05.300
set of related files, and we
need these ACID guarantees

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:07.760
for the whole data set.

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:12.620
So we created GCS data set
standard and set of libraries

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:14.720
to support that standard.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:19.280
And it uses two locations
on GCS, a data set

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:23.270
location and an index location.

00:26:23.270 --> 00:26:27.110
The Dataflow job is
configured to use the data set

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:33.490
location, each job having a
unique ID in the destination.

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:36.500
And when the Dataflow job
completes successfully,

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:39.290
we write a metadata
JSON file to the index

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:44.150
location that has the
logical name of the data set.

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:50.180
And our libraries are written
so that downstream jobs can

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:52.430
get the latest version
of the metadata

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:56.880
and retrieves the data set
from the data set location.

00:26:56.880 --> 00:26:58.550
Another decision
we had to make is

00:26:58.550 --> 00:27:01.980
how to shard our fact tables.

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:07.260
So our big data fact tables
are all partitioned by day.

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:09.470
But we also have
distinct subsets

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:12.420
of data on a non-time field.

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.830
For instance, for our
Google DoubleClick Ad data,

00:27:15.830 --> 00:27:21.350
we have the event type, such
as impression, click, or video

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:23.090
conversion.

00:27:23.090 --> 00:27:25.910
So some queries are only
interested in a subset

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:29.000
of the event types, and we
want to reduce our query costs

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:34.400
by scanning only the event type
rows needed by those queries.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:37.160
So the system we
came up with was

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:41.420
to load to multiple tables
that have the exact same field

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:46.070
schema, one table
for each event type.

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:51.060
And we created views that UNION
ALLs these tables together.

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:55.250
So queries can
just query the data

00:27:55.250 --> 00:27:59.850
that they need by choosing
the views that they query.

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:03.640
Another decision we had to
make is about time zones.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:08.130
So our decision was to use
BigQuery's timestamp type

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:10.200
in our physical tables.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:15.750
So our transformations all
convert the timestamps to UTC

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.510
before loading
into those tables.

00:28:18.510 --> 00:28:22.450
If users need to see data
in different time zones,

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:25.110
we create a view,
the view generating

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:30.600
extra DATETIME fields under
that specific time zone.

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:33.720
Another problem we
encountered during our project

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:39.720
was providing the BigQuery
table schema during loads.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:41.490
We didn't have any
problems when loading

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:46.590
the fact tables, which is the
first things that we worked on.

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:48.300
We wrote to the fact tables.

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:50.430
They were partitioned
by day, as I explained,

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:53.160
so we're loading in
WRITE_TRUNCATE mode,

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:55.950
replacing those
daily partitions.

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:59.310
And we didn't need to supply
the table schema on those loads

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:03.600
because BigQuery preserves
the schema for these partition

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:04.890
tables.

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:07.500
But then we got to the
dimension part of the project,

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:09.270
as I explained earlier.

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:11.880
We were copying those
dimension tables once a day

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:14.670
from our NiFi workflows.

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:17.850
And the dimension tables
are not partitioned,

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:20.820
so we're loading to these
unpartitioned tables

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:26.190
also in WRITE_TRUNCATE mode
to overwrite the tables.

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:29.520
We found that the table
schema was not preserved then,

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:31.980
so we just had to
change our jobs

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:37.850
to provide the table
schemas for those jobs.

00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:42.400
Next, Bhaskara will discuss
the Tableau migration

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:45.850
and our overall recommendations
from the experience we

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:49.080
had with our migration project.

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:51.290
BHASKARA PETA: Hello again.

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:58.010
I'll talk to you briefly about
our Tableau migration process

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:00.530
that we have implemented.

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:03.200
So the first thing we
did, we took an inventory

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:07.040
of all the workbooks,
tables, and reports

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:08.480
that we had to migrate.

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:13.760
And as you know, for any
company over a period of time,

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:17.816
you will create a lot of
reports which would be unused

00:30:17.816 --> 00:30:23.140
once you take about six months
or one year, or many years.

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:26.920
So we took time to identify
those unused workbooks, tables,

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:31.030
and everything and we took them
out of the migration process

00:30:31.030 --> 00:30:36.020
and that helped us with
the migration time as well.

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:39.530
We also took the priorities
from our stakeholders,

00:30:39.530 --> 00:30:41.810
which is very important.

00:30:41.810 --> 00:30:43.900
And also the conversion
was done based

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:45.970
on the priority given
by the stakeholders

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:48.880
so that they understand
when the tables are

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:51.790
coming for them to work with.

00:30:51.790 --> 00:30:55.360
And the last thing is getting
the review and signoff

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:58.900
by the owners is also critical.

00:30:58.900 --> 00:31:01.760
Next, let us look at the
conversion steps of the Tableau

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:03.510
workbooks itself.

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:05.560
And as you can see,
this is like a--

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:09.730
I hope you can see
the picture well.

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:14.280
This is like an assembly line.

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:17.680
You create a new data source
with a BigQuery connection,

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:21.900
and then rename the columns
same as existing data source.

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:25.020
Copy the calculated fields
to the new data source

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:29.520
and replace the old data source
with new BigQuery data source.

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:32.890
And then the last
type is validate.

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:34.200
So it's like an assembly line.

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:37.168
You repeat this for
each and every workbook.

00:31:37.168 --> 00:31:38.710
Now let us see some
of the challenges

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:42.910
that we faced during
the migration.

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:46.300
So for Tableau
conversion, what we did--

00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:50.380
we have migrated some
stale data to cloud to help

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:52.990
with the initial conversion.

00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:57.910
We did this mainly because we
would like both the migration

00:31:57.910 --> 00:32:01.270
and also Tableau conversion
to go in parallel so that we

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:03.730
are not waiting on one another.

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:06.010
At the same time,
make the progress

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:09.490
with the project with the
given timeline that we have.

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:12.610
But because of that, we had to
validate some of the reports

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:15.190
twice, but I think that's OK.

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:17.340
We couldn't avoid it.

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:21.200
And then as we are
doing the migration,

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:24.140
business is not going to stop
because they'll continuously

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:25.400
create the reports.

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:27.380
And it was a challenge
for us to keep up

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:30.530
with all the new workbooks that
are being created, and then

00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:32.990
bringing them into the
conversion pipeline,

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:35.960
and then plan the conversion.

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:39.680
And that added some timeline
for us, but it was a challenge

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:43.690
and we were able
to mitigate that.

00:32:43.690 --> 00:32:48.610
And then if you look at
converting all these workbooks,

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:51.310
it took some time for us
to get the hang of it.

00:32:51.310 --> 00:32:54.490
What is the best way to
migrate in a quick way?

00:32:54.490 --> 00:33:00.610
So it is like extract, convert
to Tableau, owner review,

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:02.920
and then rinse,
repeat, sign off.

00:33:02.920 --> 00:33:06.490
It is a assembly line,
as we talked before.

00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:10.770
So I'm sure some of
the users-- some of you

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:12.900
might be looking at
Tableau migration.

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:15.570
So I would like to
talk to you about some

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:17.760
of the tips about
the Tableau migration

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:22.650
that might be useful for you
based on what we thought.

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:26.880
So if you are using custom
SQLs in Tableau conversions,

00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:31.830
make sure to include the
workbook name as a comment

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:33.960
of the first line of the SQL.

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:40.050
And this will help you a lot in
identifying any SQLs that you

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:43.980
would like to tune further and
identify what is the workbook

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:45.400
name and everything.

00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:49.325
And one way to identify is
going through the audit logs.

00:33:49.325 --> 00:33:50.700
If you go through
the audit logs,

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:53.680
you can only look at the
SQLs that are running,

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:54.900
but not the workbook names.

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:59.150
So that is a way you can
get the workbook name.

00:33:59.150 --> 00:34:01.880
Pardon me, David,
for saying this.

00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:05.710
I hope you're OK
with me saying this.

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:09.500
A big no-no for
using select star

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:12.590
on any of your largest tables.

00:34:12.590 --> 00:34:14.929
You will get annoyed
pretty quickly

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:18.290
once you start using
BigQuery, and that is so--

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:22.159
especially if you are coming
from a traditional relational

00:34:22.159 --> 00:34:25.850
database because that
is how we got to know.

00:34:25.850 --> 00:34:28.550
Because the costs are going to
be pretty high if you are using

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:33.830
select star on large
tables because the cost

00:34:33.830 --> 00:34:35.620
is based on the amount
of data that you

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:37.690
are scanning in the query.

00:34:40.605 --> 00:34:41.980
There are some
standards that you

00:34:41.980 --> 00:34:44.280
have to follow for
Tableau conversions.

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:46.489
For example, for
column aliases, you

00:34:46.489 --> 00:34:51.530
have to use only letters,
numbers, and underscore.

00:34:51.530 --> 00:34:55.100
Then it should start with a
letter or underscore only,

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:57.860
and the date format
that BigQuery accepts

00:34:57.860 --> 00:35:02.380
is in a specific
format, YYYY-MM-DD.

00:35:02.380 --> 00:35:07.870
Now I would like
to show you some

00:35:07.870 --> 00:35:12.500
of the experiences from the
stakeholders, what they said

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:14.680
about BigQuery, now that
all the migration is

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:18.800
completed, in their own words.

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:21.070
These are all self-explanatory.

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:22.630
As you can see,
they thought that it

00:35:22.630 --> 00:35:26.500
is kind of a
self-service platform

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:28.090
because they were
able to automate

00:35:28.090 --> 00:35:29.540
the queries by themselves.

00:35:29.540 --> 00:35:32.140
They were able to create
the summary tables

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:35.140
without asking for any help.

00:35:35.140 --> 00:35:39.490
Also, they were able to share
the queries across the project

00:35:39.490 --> 00:35:43.810
repo, which is a great
feature that they loved it.

00:35:43.810 --> 00:35:45.950
And then BigQuery.

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:49.180
All the heavy lifting
was done using BigQuery,

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:52.720
whereas they were only
looking at smaller data

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:56.080
sets when they were working
with Python Notebooks.

00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:58.780
That is also another
great feature.

00:35:58.780 --> 00:36:02.260
And also, it is a significantly
faster processing,

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:06.700
according to what the
stakeholders have said.

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:08.900
And they were able to
work with truly big data

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:12.580
because in the past, when they
were running atomic queries,

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:16.420
sometimes it is taking forever
in some of the databases.

00:36:16.420 --> 00:36:20.080
But now, they're all getting the
data in a very, very reasonable

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:20.860
time.

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:24.780
In some cases, within a
few seconds and minutes,

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:27.040
which is great.

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:31.150
And also, another thing we did,
we empowered the stakeholders

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:32.920
in some cases.

00:36:32.920 --> 00:36:36.370
We give the ownership
of the projects

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:39.550
to some of the key stakeholders.

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.210
That helped them to be
self-sufficient in what

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:45.500
they would like to do.

00:36:45.500 --> 00:36:47.620
And they're pretty
happy with it.

00:36:47.620 --> 00:36:50.110
And also, their team was
able to do a lot of things

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:56.240
without getting any help
from other central teams.

00:36:56.240 --> 00:37:00.230
Also, as we discussed before,
we migrated the atomic data

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:03.050
wherein we introduced
the array fields.

00:37:03.050 --> 00:37:07.040
And then these array
functions actually

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:12.490
helped us with improving the
performance on the queries,

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:15.250
and also reducing the
cost on the queries

00:37:15.250 --> 00:37:18.100
that we are running
on the atomic data.

00:37:18.100 --> 00:37:22.570
And also, it has simplified
the code from a few pages

00:37:22.570 --> 00:37:24.400
to a few lines of code.

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:25.910
This is a great feature.

00:37:25.910 --> 00:37:28.810
And if you are having
any large data sets

00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:34.870
and if you can introduce these
functionalities, please do so.

00:37:34.870 --> 00:37:37.030
And also, another
interesting feature

00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:39.670
that the customers have
liked was the integration

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:41.440
of Google Sheets with BigQuery.

00:37:41.440 --> 00:37:43.510
And this one was
definitely liked

00:37:43.510 --> 00:37:46.900
by some of the stakeholders
in our company.

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:49.210
Now let us look at some
of the big wins for us.

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:52.070
What are the big wins
that we have seen?

00:37:52.070 --> 00:37:55.540
As you have seen in the previous
slides, happy stakeholders.

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:57.430
What else we need?

00:37:57.430 --> 00:37:59.410
That's what we are
all striving for.

00:37:59.410 --> 00:38:01.280
We want to make the
customers happy,

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:04.660
and that is what happened
with the BigQuery migration.

00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:07.210
Significant
improvement with SLAs,

00:38:07.210 --> 00:38:10.360
and we have seen a
lot of improvement.

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:13.700
And also, for Super Bowl LIII
just a couple of months ago,

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:16.240
we were able to process
the largest data

00:38:16.240 --> 00:38:20.350
sets during that event
without any problem,

00:38:20.350 --> 00:38:25.780
without any limitations on the
database sizes, which is great.

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:28.060
Our operations team,
they were able to reduce

00:38:28.060 --> 00:38:31.090
the footprint of the
data center because we

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:35.980
were able to shut down
Teradata and Hadoop.

00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:39.070
And then, our engineers
are able to focus

00:38:39.070 --> 00:38:42.520
on the real problems rather than
some of the day-to-day issues

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:47.200
that we have been seeing with
our on-premise infrastructure.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:50.620
Also, we were able to
reduce the number of tables

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:52.995
from on-premise to cloud.

00:38:52.995 --> 00:38:54.370
We were able to
reduce, I think--

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:57.760
at least about 50% of the tables
we were able to eliminate.

00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:01.270
Because as I said before,
over a period of time,

00:39:01.270 --> 00:39:04.510
you create the tables
and we never use them

00:39:04.510 --> 00:39:08.380
because the functionality have
changed or the project got

00:39:08.380 --> 00:39:10.510
dumped out and
nobody is using it.

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:13.780
And we never know
that, so we took time

00:39:13.780 --> 00:39:15.970
to identify those
tables and then

00:39:15.970 --> 00:39:19.000
remove them from the migration.

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:22.060
Reduced dependency is
increased productivity,

00:39:22.060 --> 00:39:25.510
and that is what we have
seen with the cloud migration

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:30.300
because everybody was able to do
a lot of the tasks on their own

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:34.180
with less dependency
on the central teams.

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:36.540
Now that we are
coming to the end,

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:39.280
I would like you to leave
with some of the takeaways

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:41.398
that we learned
from this migration.

00:39:41.398 --> 00:39:42.940
Probably they would
be useful for you

00:39:42.940 --> 00:39:46.870
if you are planning on
migrating data to the cloud.

00:39:46.870 --> 00:39:49.810
Training, training, training.

00:39:49.810 --> 00:39:50.800
Don't get us wrong.

00:39:50.800 --> 00:39:53.440
We did train our engineers.

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:58.800
But at the end, when we did the
retrospective of the project,

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:02.470
we thought that we could
do much more training

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:03.710
for our engineers.

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:06.250
And also, if you are
working in a small team,

00:40:06.250 --> 00:40:08.440
make sure to cross-train
your engineers

00:40:08.440 --> 00:40:12.370
across different products,
and that will help a lot.

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:16.180
Also, if you can, train
on your own company data

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:18.970
rather than using
superstore data.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:23.380
Because the analysts will get
early on hands-on experience

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:25.560
with your own data.

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:28.280
And since you're migrating
to a new platform,

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:30.650
new software, new
processes, make

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:36.320
sure you have that feedback loop
to identify the improvements

00:40:36.320 --> 00:40:38.540
that you need to make
on the new processes

00:40:38.540 --> 00:40:40.960
that you are implementing.

00:40:40.960 --> 00:40:43.010
Because sometimes,
we ignore that,

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:45.250
but I think what we
have seen is it's always

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:47.670
good to have that feedback.

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:49.660
And the task force.

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:51.970
This is something that
worked very, very well for us

00:40:51.970 --> 00:40:56.290
because they're
invested in the process.

00:40:56.290 --> 00:40:59.350
They have been working with us
and we are working with them

00:40:59.350 --> 00:41:02.920
all through the migration
to make any major decisions

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:05.140
around cloud migration.

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:07.990
And they're happy
to be part of it.

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:12.670
And it helps them to know
how things are changing

00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:14.450
and how they should be prepared.

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:19.000
And also, it helps them to
get early access to the data

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:25.210
and also help identifying
the issues early on.

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:28.600
And then, take time
to discover and retire

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:32.000
any obsolete
processes or tables.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:34.420
Even though sometimes,
it is time-consuming

00:41:34.420 --> 00:41:37.420
because it took some time for
us to go through all the logs

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:38.540
and everything.

00:41:38.540 --> 00:41:42.520
But it will help you
in the migration time

00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:44.350
and the overall
process so that you

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:47.920
can remove the clutter
in the new platform.

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:52.210
The next one is try to migrate
most recent data to the cloud

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:53.500
first.

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:56.750
In our case, that
is what we did.

00:41:56.750 --> 00:42:02.560
And it helped the analysts to
give early access to the data.

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:06.520
And also, they were able to play
with our own data in the cloud

00:42:06.520 --> 00:42:11.110
and help us to give us the
feedback on identifying

00:42:11.110 --> 00:42:13.450
the problems early on.

00:42:13.450 --> 00:42:16.380
That is a great
thing that helped us.

00:42:16.380 --> 00:42:18.910
And when we did that, we
also copied the dimension

00:42:18.910 --> 00:42:21.790
data to the cloud so that
they can get the entire data

00:42:21.790 --> 00:42:24.590
set to play with.

00:42:24.590 --> 00:42:25.970
Query costs.

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:28.290
This is very, very important.

00:42:28.290 --> 00:42:31.220
And even now, we have been
seeing some of the areas

00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:34.830
where we can improve
in our company.

00:42:34.830 --> 00:42:39.420
But what we did, especially
with our cloud migration,

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:43.610
we have created
some alerts early on

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:47.705
to alert us if anybody
is running a query--

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:54.170
if the cost is exceeding
a certain limit,

00:42:54.170 --> 00:42:56.390
we'll get an email or alert.

00:42:56.390 --> 00:43:00.200
So it's like training the
users to tell them, hey, this

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:04.340
is the query you ran and
this query cost you $1,000.

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:07.740
So please be mindful
because we had queries--

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:10.790
initially, when we started
working with BigQuery,

00:43:10.790 --> 00:43:14.090
there were users who would
run the queries worth $8,000.

00:43:14.090 --> 00:43:16.610
You may not believe
it, but that is true.

00:43:16.610 --> 00:43:21.590
But pretty quickly, we
were able to contain that,

00:43:21.590 --> 00:43:25.310
and then everybody now is
able to run the queries very

00:43:25.310 --> 00:43:27.770
effectively, and those
alerts helped us.

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:30.700
And we are continuously
improving on how well

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:33.350
we can improve that process.

00:43:33.350 --> 00:43:37.460
Also, our strategy with
go live is sequential.

00:43:37.460 --> 00:43:41.630
And that helped us a lot
because it gave us momentum.

00:43:41.630 --> 00:43:43.970
Once we go live with
first business unit,

00:43:43.970 --> 00:43:46.850
it gave us the momentum
for the rest of the groups

00:43:46.850 --> 00:43:48.800
to go live because
they have seen

00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:51.350
other people, other
business units, going live.

00:43:51.350 --> 00:43:55.340
And that helped
us and also them.

00:43:55.340 --> 00:43:56.870
So that's what I would suggest.

00:43:56.870 --> 00:44:00.770
If you have many,
many brands, I think

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:03.060
you can take that approach.

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:07.490
And as you have seen, this is
a very, very complex project

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:10.950
over many, many brands with a
lot of different priorities.

00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:15.890
And we have a great team
and excellent stakeholders,

00:44:15.890 --> 00:44:18.860
and that is how we
were able to do it.

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:20.810
That's all we
have, and thank you

00:44:20.810 --> 00:44:23.330
for joining our session today.

00:44:23.330 --> 00:44:26.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

